          early: `Henry Ford worked at a company that made electric light for Detroit. At night he built a little car in a shed. It had four bicycle wheels and a small engine. In June 1896 he drove it down the street. Later he started the Ford Motor Company.`,
          elementary: `In the 1890s Henry Ford worked at the Edison company in Detroit, which made electricity for the city. In his spare time he built a small gasoline carriage with bicycle wheels, the Quadricycle, and drove it through the streets in June 1896. His first car companies did not last. Then he built a fast racing car, the 999, and a famous driver named Barney Oldfield raced it. On 16 June 1903 Ford started the Ford Motor Company, with money from investors who included the Dodge brothers.`,
          middle: `Ford rose to chief engineer at the Edison Illuminating Company in Detroit while building gasoline engines in a shed behind his rented house. He finished the Quadricycle, a light gasoline carriage, in 1896 and test-drove it on 4 June. The early companies built around his cars did not last: the Detroit Automobile Company was dissolved in 1901 and the Henry Ford Company, formed that year, was short-lived. Ford later wrote that investors had treated the first company as a money-making scheme and sold very few cars. After the 999 racer, driven by Barney Oldfield in 1902, Ford Motor Company was incorporated on 16 June 1903 with $28,000 in capital.`,
          high: `Ford's path from backyard experiments to a viable company ran through failure and racing. He was chief engineer at the Edison Illuminating Company by 1893 and test-drove the Quadricycle on 4 June 1896. The Detroit Automobile Company (1899, dissolved in January 1901) and the Henry Ford Company (November 1901) both failed; Ford later wrote that investors saw only a way to make money and sold very few cars. The 999, built in 1902 and driven by Barney Oldfield, was in Ford's own later words meant to show that he could build a fast motor car. Ford Motor Company was incorporated on 16 June 1903 with $28,000 in capital, and John and Horace Dodge, who supplied parts, were among the investors. Ford's own book dates his first motor car to 1892 and its satisfactory running to 1893; the dossier marks that as contested against the 1896 Quadricycle.`,
