          early: `The Model T was a plain, tough car. It was made for ordinary families, not just rich people. Many families could buy one. People loved to ride in it.`,
          elementary: `The Model T went on sale on 1 October 1908. At that time cars were mostly for rich people, but Henry Ford wanted to build one for ordinary families. The Model T was simple and strong, with a small four-cylinder engine. When Ford said he would make just one kind of car, people said his company would be out of business in six months. Instead, the Model T sold so well that the price fell to about $260, and about fifteen million were built.`,
          middle: `Before the Model T, Ford wrote, a motor car was still thought of as a luxury, and makers sold the idea of a "pleasure car." The Model T, introduced on 1 October 1908 at about $825, was different: a four-cylinder, 20-horsepower car made with strong vanadium steel, simple enough to run and repair, and able to burn gasoline, kerosene or ethanol. Ford said his sales staff predicted failure in six months. Instead sales of more than ten thousand cars in 1908-1909 convinced him he needed a bigger factory, and prices fell to about $260 by the mid-1920s as volume rose. About fifteen million Model Ts were built before production ended in May 1927.`,
          high: `The Model T was both a machine and a statement about who a car was for. As an object it was modest: a 177-cubic-inch four-cylinder engine of 20 horsepower, a design credited chiefly to Joseph Galamb with Eugene Farkas and C. Harold Wills under Ford, built of vanadium steel and able to run on gasoline, kerosene or ethanol. As an idea it reversed the usual logic of the market. Ford wrote that a motor car was still regarded as a luxury and the industry sold the "pleasure car"; his salesmen told him that a low-priced single model would put the company out of business in six months. The opposite happened, though the dossier treats Ford's later telling as his own account: the commercial record shows an iterative campaign of cost-cutting, with prices falling from about $825 to about $260 and some fifteen million cars built by 1927.`,
          university: `The Model T should be read as two things that are easily confused, an engineering artifact and a retrospective legend. As engineering it is a conservative, repairable design whose specifications (177 cubic inches, 20 horsepower, vanadium steel, multi-fuel tolerance) are documented in secondary summaries, with design credit divided among Galamb, Farkas, Wills and Ford. As legend it is Ford's own narrative, written with Crowther in 1922, in which a luxury trade is overturned by a single model for the multitude over the objection of salesmen. That narrative is probable but self-serving: the sales figure he cites (over ten thousand cars in 1908-1909) is his own, and his account of a single announced strategy elides the firm's pricing experiments. The price and volume trajectory ($825 to about $260; some 15 million cars) is the better evidence of the achievement, and the one scholars such as Hounshell tie to the production system rather than to the car alone.`,
