        text: {
          early: `Ford built a huge new factory at Highland Park, near Detroit. Inside, the cars moved along a line, and each worker did one small job. Cars could be made much faster this way. More families could afford them.`,
          elementary: `In 1910 the Ford company opened a giant factory at Highland Park, near Detroit. In 1913, Ford's engineers put the work on a moving assembly line. Instead of workers walking around one car, the car moved past the workers, and each person did one small job. A car chassis that once took about twelve and a half hours to build took about ninety-three minutes by early 1914. Many people worked together to invent the line, and people still argue about who deserves the most credit.`,
          middle: `Ford wrote that the sales of 1908-1909 made him buy sixty acres at Highland Park, which was then thought to be far out in the country. The huge plant, designed by Albert Kahn's firm, opened in 1910. During 1913 assembly moved onto lines in stages: flywheel magnetos around April, then whole chassis by August. A chassis that had taken roughly twelve and a half hours took about ninety-three minutes by early 1914. Ford managers and engineers developed the line together, among them Clarence Avery, Peter Martin, Charles Sorensen and C. Harold Wills, and historians still argue about who deserves credit.`,
          high: `Highland Park (opened 1910, designed by Albert Kahn's firm) was the place where the Model T's volume was matched to a new way of working. The moving assembly line came in stages in 1913: flywheel magnetos moved onto a line around 1 April, and by August the line was extended to complete chassis. The result, a chassis labor time cut from roughly 12.5 hours to 93 minutes by early 1914, is confirmed by The Henry Ford and by Ford's own book. Individual credit is contested: Avery, Martin, Sorensen and Wills are all named, and the line is better understood as a collective engineering achievement than as one inventor's idea. The pace came at a cost, since the repetitive work drove extremely high turnover, which prepared the ground for the Five Dollar Day.`,
          university: `David Hounshell's From the American System to Mass Production (1984) is the standard scholarly frame: Highland Park was the end of a long technological development, from interchangeable parts to continuous-flow layout, in which Ford's firm was the decisive actor, not the product of a single invention. The ledger therefore marks individual credit (Avery, Martin, Sorensen, Wills; Sorensen's later claims) as contested. The staged introduction of the line and the reduction of chassis time from about 12.5 hours to 93 minutes are documented by contemporary observers, and the 1915 Arnold and Faurote study of the Ford shops, summarised by The Henry Ford, records them. The site questions are smaller but telling: Ford's book says he bought sixty acres, while a modern summary gives about a hundred acres at the plant's height, a difference best explained by expansion, which is an inference. The line's social consequence, extremely high turnover, is part of the explanation for the 1914 wage reform.`,
        },
